---
import { useTranslations, getLangFromUrl } from '@/i18n/utils'
const lang = getLangFromUrl(Astro.url)
const t = useTranslations(lang)
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'
import CenteringWrapper from '../atoms/centering-wrapper.astro'
import HeadingTextBlock from '../organisms/heading-text-block.astro'
import Waves from '../atoms/waves.astro'
---

<Layout title='About'>
	<Waves class='h-35 w-full' />
	<CenteringWrapper className='flex-col'>
		<HeadingTextBlock headingText='About me'>
			<p>
				Before I even knew terms like layout or design, I was fascinated by
				magazines and fanzines - especially those focused on music. It wasn’t
				just the content that drew me in, but also the form: the way text,
				photography, and graphics come together to create a cohesive whole. As a
				teenager, I tried to create my own fanzine. Today I cringe a little
				thinking about its imperfections, but the satisfaction of putting it
				together was very real - and, in many ways, that hasn’t changed. Years
				later, I found the same satisfaction in web development: I truly enjoy
				translating the language of design into code. I now work mainly with
				Astro.js, React/Next.js, and headless CMS, building interfaces that are
				not only functional, but also visually consistent
			</p>
		</HeadingTextBlock>
		<HeadingTextBlock headingText='hobbies'>
			<p>{t('about.hobbies')}</p>
		</HeadingTextBlock>
		<HeadingTextBlock headingText='music'>
			<p>{t('about.music')}</p>
		</HeadingTextBlock>
		<HeadingTextBlock headingText='film'>
			<p>{t('about.film')}</p>
		</HeadingTextBlock>
	</CenteringWrapper>
</Layout>
